# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Install to /etc/radar/analyst.env (root-owned, 0640, group guardian).
#
#   sudo install -m 0640 -o root -g guardian analyst.env.example /etc/radar/analyst.env
#
# Everything here is deny-by-default. An empty file gives you a service that
# runs, reads nothing, posts nothing, and says so on every tick -- which is the
# resting state, not a broken one.

# Where the reply log, the cursor and the spend ledger live. The unit grants
# write access to this path and to nothing else.
RADAR_ANALYST_DIR=/home/guardian/radar/data/analyst

# --- The credential ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# BOTH or NEITHER. A token with no user id has nothing to poll and a user id
# with no token cannot poll it, so a half-configured instance is treated as
# unconfigured: it reads nothing and posts nothing.
#
# THIS MAKES THE ACCOUNT READ, NOT SPEAK. With these set and nothing else, the
# service polls real mentions, answers them, and writes every answer to the log
# where you can read it beside the fact sheet it was built from. It says nothing
# in public. That is the state the first hundred replies are meant to be read in.
#
# RADAR_X_BEARER=
# RADAR_X_USER_ID=

# --- The credential that lets it POST ----------------------------------------
#
# ALL FOUR, and they are not the bearer.
#
# `POST /2/tweets` **refuses an app-only bearer token.** It requires user
# context -- OAuth 1.0a, or OAuth 2.0 with `tweet.write` (checked against
# docs.x.com, 2026-09-05). So reading and posting need two different credentials,
# and an instance with only the bearer is one that answers every mention and
# delivers none of them. The daemon says exactly that on startup rather than
# leaving it to be discovered on the first real reply.
#
# From the developer portal, under Keys and tokens:
#
#   RADAR_X_API_KEY        = "API Key"
#   RADAR_X_API_SECRET     = "API Key Secret"
#   RADAR_X_ACCESS_TOKEN   = "Access Token"
#   RADAR_X_ACCESS_SECRET  = "Access Token Secret"
#
# **App permissions must be "Read and write" BEFORE the access token is
# generated.** A token minted under "Read" keeps those permissions for its whole
# life and fails to post with a 403 that does not explain itself. If in doubt,
# change the permission and regenerate.
#
# Three of four reads as none, so a half-finished edit leaves an instance that
# cannot speak rather than one that speaks wrongly.
#
# RADAR_X_API_KEY=
# RADAR_X_API_SECRET=
# RADAR_X_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# RADAR_X_ACCESS_SECRET=

# --- Speaking in public ------------------------------------------------------
#
# THIS is the switch that makes the account public, and it is deliberately not
# the credential. Exactly the word `on`; anything else -- including `true`, `1`
# and `yes` -- is silence, because a ceiling spelled wrongly must not read as
# permission and this is a ceiling on speech.
#
# It was one switch until 2026-09-05. Pasting a token turned a silent instance
# into a public account in a single step, and there was no way to satisfy the
# launch gate -- a hundred replies read beside their fact sheets -- without
# publishing the hundred.
#
# Two wrong figures were found in the reply path on 2026-09-04 alone: a cost
# 6.7x too high, and a charge signed as a gain. Both were found by reading real
# output. The first hundred replies are where the next one gets found, and that
# should not happen in public.
#
# The daemon prints which of the three states it is in on every start.
#
# RADAR_X_PUBLISH=on

# --- Prices ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ALL FOUR or the service answers nothing. There are no defaults, and that is
# deliberate: a default price is a spending decision made by whoever wrote the
# code, and this repository has an ADR's worth of regret about deciding on an
# unverified number.
#
# Micro-USD, whole numbers, so a price is written exactly:
#
#   $0.001 =  1000        $0.005 =  5000
#   $0.010 = 10000        $0.200 = 200000
#
# Two of these are not yet verified against a real invoice and are the reason
# this file asks rather than assumes: whether a mentions poll bills as an owned
# read ($0.001) or a standard one ($0.005), and whether a summoned reply
# carrying a URL is $0.010 or $0.200. Put in what you were actually charged.
#
# RADAR_X_PRICE_MENTION_READ=1000
# RADAR_X_PRICE_POST_READ=5000
# RADAR_X_PRICE_REPLY=10000
# RADAR_MODEL_PER_CALL_USD_MICRO=2000

# --- The ceiling -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BOTH or the budget is CLOSED and every call is refused. In dollars, because an
# operator writing a daily cap thinks in dollars. A value that will not parse is
# also closed: a typo in a spending ceiling must not read as permission.
#
# RADAR_ANALYST_DAILY_USD=5.00
# RADAR_ANALYST_PER_CALL_USD=0.25

# --- Who gets answered -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Unset means ZERO, and zero means refuse. These are what stop one account
# emptying the day's budget, and what stop the same coin being answered twice.
#
# The per-summoner cap is charged when a mention is ADMITTED -- that is when
# the chain is read, which is the cost -- and the global cap when a reply is
# actually sent. So one account gets at most this many dossiers a day whether
# or not anything posts, and a publisher outage cannot spend the account's cap.
#
# RADAR_ANALYST_PER_SUMMONER_DAILY=3
# RADAR_ANALYST_GLOBAL_DAILY=50
# RADAR_ANALYST_DEDUPE_SECONDS=3600

# --- The chain ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The RPC endpoint the dossier is read from. Direct, never the x402 lane: a
# mention is answered into a live thread and on-chain settlement adds hundreds
# of milliseconds before a response returns (AGENTS.md rule 7).
#
# `RADAR_RPC`, and this file said `RADAR_RPC_URL` until 2026-09-06 -- which is
# the payout binary's variable, copied from `payout.env.example`. The analyst
# reads `RADAR_RPC` (`RpcClient::from_vars`), so an operator who followed this
# file set a variable nothing read and got the **free public endpoint** instead:
# rate-limited, and unable to reach the history a launch lookup needs. Nothing
# would have said so. That is exactly the failure `RpcClient::endpoint` was made
# public to prevent, and it arrived in the one place that check cannot see.
#
# Unset is not an error. It is the free public node, which works and throttles.
#
# RADAR_RPC=

# --- The voice ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Optional. Without it every reply is the deterministic template, which is a
# working product rather than a degraded one -- it just has no jokes in it.
#
# Note the model must be the metered API path, never the subscription CLI: that
# one is marked private use only, and this account is public.
#
# TWO PROVIDERS, ONE AT A TIME. Set exactly one key. Setting two is refused at
# start-up with both named, rather than resolved by whichever branch the code
# checks first -- an operator who believes they have moved to the other vendor
# while still paying the first is the failure that refusal exists to stop.
#
# The other four variables are shared, so switching vendors is the key, the
# endpoint, the model name and the two prices. Nothing is rebuilt and nothing is
# redeployed: edit this file and restart the unit.
#
# OpenAI, Chat Completions:
#
# RADAR_MODEL_OPENAI_KEY=
# RADAR_MODEL_ENDPOINT=https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions
# RADAR_MODEL_NAME=
#
# Anthropic, Messages -- claude-sonnet-5, claude-opus-5, claude-haiku-4-5:
#
# RADAR_MODEL_API_KEY=
# RADAR_MODEL_ENDPOINT=https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages
# RADAR_MODEL_NAME=
#
# Prices, whichever key is set. Micro-dollars per MILLION tokens, so a rate card
# reading $3.00 per million in and $15.00 per million out is 3000000 and
# 15000000. Read them off the vendor's own page rather than from here: a price
# written into this file is a number that goes stale silently, in the direction
# of under-counting. Both are required, and a value that will not parse is a
# missing one -- there is no default, because a default price is a spending
# decision made by whoever typed it.
#
# RADAR_MODEL_PRICE_IN=
# RADAR_MODEL_PRICE_OUT=
#
# Since 2026-09-06 every model call is reserved before it is made and settled at
# what the provider reported, against the same daily budget as the X costs -- so
# a day of replies cannot spend past RADAR_ANALYST_DAILY_USD, whichever lane
# asked. A provider that answers without reporting its usage is charged
# RADAR_MODEL_PER_CALL_USD_MICRO above, never nothing.

# --- The analyst's own token -------------------------------------------------
#
# ADR 0013 constraint 5. When set, a price or market-cap fact about this mint is
# dropped from the fact sheet before the model sees it, and the reply says so.
# Everything else about the token is stated on the same rule as any other coin.
#
# Leave it unset or blank until the token exists: no token is special, and that
# is the correct configuration rather than a missing one. A value that is NOT an
# address stops the daemon -- a misspelt mint would silently switch the rule off
# for the real token, which is the one thing this line exists to prevent. The
# daemon prints which state it is in on every start.
#
# RADAR_SELF_MINT=

# --- Telegram: the free lane -------------------------------------------------
#
# Design 0009 L5. X is the public record and the contest; Telegram is where the
# same question costs nothing to answer. Same parser, same gate shape, same fact
# path, same two checks -- only the transport differs. Answers go to
# telegram.jsonl beside replies.jsonl, and NOTHING that scores the contest reads
# that file: a Telegram answer is kept out of the record by where it is written,
# not by a flag.
#
# A DIFFERENT BOT from the alert channel's. The alert bot talks to you; this one
# talks to strangers. A shared token would let a stranger's message land in the
# alert chat. Make a new one in BotFather and paste only its token here.
#
# Unset or blank: nothing is read from Telegram and nothing is sent, and the
# daemon says so on start.
#
# RADAR_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
#
# The token alone READS and answers into telegram.jsonl only. This makes it
# reply in the chat. Exactly `on`; anything else is off.
#
# RADAR_TELEGRAM_PUBLISH=on
#
# Its own caps, separate from X's. Unset means ZERO, and zero means refuse. The
# per-summoner cap is charged on admission (that is when the chain is read);
# the global one when a reply is actually sent. There is no platform bill here,
# so the global cap is a ceiling on model calls and RPC, not on posts.
#
# RADAR_TELEGRAM_PER_SUMMONER_DAILY=20
# RADAR_TELEGRAM_GLOBAL_DAILY=500
# RADAR_TELEGRAM_DEDUPE_SECONDS=3600

# --- The account's own posts -------------------------------------------------
#
# Two appointments (design 0009 section 7). The week closes on the first tick
# after Monday 00:00 UTC: the week's replies are scored on their public
# metrics, the record is written to the contest directory, and the account
# posts the result with the winner's coin torn down as a reply. Every day at
# 12:00 UTC it posts "seven days later" from the file `radar seven-days-later`
# wrote (see radar-seven-days.timer).
#
# A top-level post is a different product from a reply and X bills it
# differently. ALL FIVE prices are required now; the fifth is this one. Put in
# what you were actually charged.
#
# RADAR_X_PRICE_POST=15000
#
# Where the Telegram lane posts its copies of the two. Unset means the lane
# answers people and posts nothing on its own. A chat id or an @channel name
# the bot is an admin of.
#
# RADAR_TELEGRAM_CHANNEL=
